// LAINFLAGS: --engine=ir-full
// The CONSTRUCTOR spelling of a struct, which proved nothing while the field-by-field spelling
// of the identical struct proved everything. Two things had to meet for that:
//
//   · `Point(3, 4)` builds an IR_STRUCT_NEW and stores it WHOLE, so there is no per-field
//     IR_STORE — the fields are the store value's OPERANDS and have to be read from there.
//   · computing an address does not change what is at it. The transfer's default forgets every
//     instruction result, which is right for a fresh value and wrong for a field_ptr: it NAMES
//     a cell that already holds something. Order made it visible — `p.x = 3` creates the
//     field_ptr BEFORE the store so the forget was harmless, while a constructor stores first
//     and reads later, so the READ's own field_ptr wiped what the store had just put there.
type Point { x i32, y i32 }

proc main() i32 {
    var p = Point(3, 4)
    return p.x + p.y - 7
}
